The modules compiler/corpus_test.go reads. Nothing here is compiled into the
module, and nothing here is imported: these are files the tests open.

The 3GPP specifications are not here. They are whole protocols rather than
fixtures for one package, so they sit in testdata/3gpp at the root of the
repository with a PROVENANCE of their own.

otp/
    360 modules from the test suite Erlang/OTP uses on its own ASN.1 compiler,
    taken from https://github.com/erlang/otp (lib/asn1/test) at master on
    2026-09-24. Directory structure is flattened into the file names, since
    nothing here reads a path.

    Licence: Apache 2.0. otp/LICENSE is that licence, kept with the files it
    covers.

    The most varied of the three corpora: X.500's directory services, the
    intelligent network protocols, and a long tail of modules written to
    exercise one corner each. Some are meant to be rejected - OTP tests its
    compiler's error messages with them - and unlike asn1c's, their names do
    not say which. That is why the test asserts a count rather than a verdict
    per file.

asn1c/
    This project's own modules, under this project's own licence. The name
    says what the parser must do with each:

        <subject>-ok.asn1     must parse
        <subject>-nok.asn1    must not

    A file with neither suffix fails the test, because the convention is the
    test: a module whose verdict is not written down is one nobody can tell
    has stopped doing what it was written for.

    The idea is asn1c's - that a corpus should carry its own verdict rather
    than leave it in a list somewhere - and it is the reason that corpus was
    worth reading. The spelling is shorter here: asn1c writes -OK, -SE and
    -NP, and the middle one separates a semantic error from a correct module,
    which is a line a parser cannot see at all. Both of those must parse, so
    two marks do the work of one.

    Names are the area then the subject, in words - module-header-ok.asn1,
    constraint-binds-inward-ok.asn1 - so that a listing groups itself. No
    numbers: a number says nothing, and two people adding a module on the same
    day collide on it.

    The seven -nok modules are refused by decision, and each says in its first
    lines which decision and why, with the section of lib/review.md that
    records it. If one starts parsing, a decision was reversed without anybody
    meaning to - which is the test the borrowed corpora cannot give, since
    they say what some other compiler accepts and not what this one decided.

    The sample modules this project kept in samples/ are here too, with a
    sample- prefix, except builtin.asn1, which defines the reserved words DATE
    and DURATION and is therefore -nok for the reason it says itself. Keeping
    them in two places was keeping two answers to one question.
